A component can be an ordinary value containing its HTML and the
stylesheets it needs. A const function constructs that value. Another const
function combines several parts into one page.

This is the intended `@html` component API, **not yet a shipped package
surface**. In particular, const functions and combined stylesheet
contributions still need their planned implementation.

### Compose a documentation page

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- docs/functions/@entry.moth
    -- Proposed @html component helpers

    @html HtmlPart, html_page
    @components navbar, docs_sidebar, docs_topic
    @introduction content as introduction

    article #= HtmlPart(
        body = [introduction]
    )

    page #= html_page({
        navbar(),
        docs_sidebar("Documentation"),
        docs_topic("Functions", article)
    })

    [$in title:Functions]
    [$in description:Function declarations and calls.]

    -- Proposed multi-resource stylesheet contribution.
    [$in stylesheets:[page.stylesheets]]

    [page.body]
]

The entry owns the final document. Each component returns its body and
stylesheet references, while `html_page()` assembles the parts in order.
The page passes dependencies to the builder instead of letting a nested
template insert into a distant `head` or `stylesheets` slot.

A small page can skip `html_page()` and write its main content directly:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    @components sidebar

    [sidebar]

    [$md:
        # Getting started

        Write an ordinary Moth program first.
    ]
]

Use the abstraction where it saves work. A component system should not make
a simple page harder to read.

### Reuse a component

A sidebar component can return the same markup for many pages with different
headings. Its implementation lives in a shared package, alongside
`sidebar.css`. The stylesheet is a tracked resource, not a string URL
assembled by the component.

Components may also contain local templates with named `$slot`s. A
`$in` contribution fills the receiving template's immediate slots. It
does not search ancestors or send implicit builder messages.

See @../../templates (Templates) for slot and wrapper composition.
